"A diagrammatic approach to the three-page index | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2601.12846)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; @misc{yoo2026diagrammaticapproachthreepageindex, title={A diagrammatic approach to the three-page index}, author={Hyungkee Yoo}, year={2026}, eprint={2601.12846}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.GT}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.12846}, }\nSubmitted 19 Jan 2026\n\n# A diagrammatic approach to the three-page index\n\nHY\n\nHyungkee Yoo\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe three-page index $\\alpha_3(L)$α 3​(L) is an invariant that measures the complexity of representing a link $L$L in a three-page book. It is known that $\\alpha_3(L)$α 3​(L) admits a linear upper bound in terms of the crossing number, with equality realized by the Hopf link. In this paper, we investigate the equality case of this bound from a diagrammatic viewpoint. Starting from a reduced link diagram, we construct three-page presentations via binding circles arising as boundaries of suitable contractible subcomplexes of the induced cell decomposition of the $2$2-sphere. This approach allows a refined control of the number of arcs in the resulting three-page presentation. As a consequence, we prove that for any non-split, nontrivial link $L$L other than the Hopf link, $$ \\alpha_3(L)\\le 3c(L)-1, $$α 3​(L)≤3 c(L)−1, and hence characterize completely the links for which $\\alpha_3(L)=3c(L)$α 3​(L)=3 c(L).\n\n## BibTeX\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{yoo2026diagrammaticapproachthreepageindex, title={A diagrammatic approach to the three-page index}, author={Hyungkee Yoo}, year={2026}, eprint={2601.12846}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.GT}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.12846}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBasis Number and Pathwidth | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2601.14095)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: last month; @misc{miraftab2026basisnumberpathwidth, title={Basis Number and Pathwidth}, author={Babak Miraftab and Pat Morin and Yelena Yuditsky}, year={2026}, eprint={2601.14095}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.14095}, }\nSubmitted 20 Jan 2026\n\n# Basis Number and Pathwidth\n\nBM\n\nBabak MiraftabPat Morin\n\nYY\n\nYelena Yuditsky\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe prove two results relating the basis number of a graph $G$G to path decompositions of $G$G. Our first result shows that the basis number of a graph is at most four times its pathwidth. Our second result shows that, if a graph $G$G has a path decomposition with adhesions of size at most $k$k in which the graph induced by each bag has basis number at most $b$b, then $G$G has basis number at most $b+O(k\\log^2 k)$b+O(k lo g 2 k). The first result, combined with recent work of Geniet and Giocanti shows that the basis number of a graph is bounded by a polynomial function of its treewidth. The second result (also combined with the work of Geniet and Giocanti) shows that every $K_t$K t​-minor-free graph has a basis number bounded by a polynomial function of $t$t.\n\nView Paper\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{miraftab2026basisnumberpathwidth, title={Basis Number and Pathwidth}, author={Babak Miraftab and Pat Morin and Yelena Yuditsky}, year={2026}, eprint={2601.14095}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.14095}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Basis Number and Pathwidth. (https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-2601-14095.html)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; DOI: 10.48550/ARXIV.2601.14095\n\n# \"Basis Number and Pathwidth.\"\n\n  * help us\n\n    * How can I correct errors in dblp?\n    * contact dblp\n\nBabak Miraftab, Pat Morin, Yelena Yuditsky (2026)\n\n  *   * Image: use mirror server\n\nDagstuhl\n\n\n## Details and statistics\n\n  * show external API response\n\n   